---
// The explanation below the Lab: the data, the symbols, how a forest is
// grown, why votes help, choosing T, and the model in short. Static HTML;
// formulas are rendered by KaTeX at build time.
import { renderTex } from '../../lib/katex';
import { equations } from './equations';

const tex = Object.fromEntries(equations.map((e) => [e.id, renderTex(e.tex)]));
---

<div class="explain">
  <section aria-labelledby="forest-data">
    <h2 id="forest-data">What you are looking at</h2>
    <p>
      Each shape is a seed in a tray. Across the graph is how warm its soil was;
      up the graph is how much water it got each day. Circles sprouted and
      triangles stayed dry. Seeds sprout when warmth and water together are
      enough, so the border between them runs on a slant.
    </p>
    <p>
      The diamond is a new seed. Its colour is the forest's answer, and the
      shading shows the answer everywhere else: stronger where more trees agree.
      Below the graph, every tree is drawn as a small map. The numbers are made
      up, and <strong>Refresh Data</strong> shows another tray of 30 seeds.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="forest-symbols">
    <h2 id="forest-symbols">What each symbol means</h2>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <div>
        <dt>q</dt>
        <dd>The new seed, the one we want an answer for.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>hₜ(q)</dt>
        <dd>The answer of tree number t.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-line">T</dt>
        <dd>How many trees vote. It is always odd, so the vote never ties.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>n</dt>
        <dd>How many seeds there are to learn from.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>ŷ</dt>
        <dd>The forest's answer: sprouts or stays dry.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="forest-how">
    <h2 id="forest-how">How a forest is grown</h2>
    <p>
      A random forest is many small decision trees that vote. If every tree
      learned from the same seeds in the same way, they would all be the same
      tree, and the vote would add nothing. So each tree gets two kinds of
      chance:
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Its own sample.</strong> Each tree draws n seeds at random,
        putting each one back after drawing it. Some seeds come twice or more,
        and some not at all. Pick a tree to see its sample on the big graph.
        <div class="formula" set:html={tex.bootstrap} />
        <div class="formula" set:html={tex.unseen} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Its own questions.</strong> At each question, the tree may only
        look at one feature, picked at random: temperature or water. It then
        finds the best threshold on that feature.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A vote.</strong> For a new seed, each tree gives its answer, and
        the most common answer wins.
        <div class="formula" set:html={tex.vote} />
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="forest-why">
    <h2 id="forest-why">Why many trees beat one</h2>
    <p>
      One tree can only cut the chart with straight lines across and up, so a
      slanted border becomes a rough staircase. Each tree's staircase is
      different, and its mistakes are in different places. When they vote, the
      mistakes tend to cancel out, and the forest's border follows the slant
      more closely than any single tree.
    </p>
    <p>
      Odd seeds near the border fool some trees but not others: a tree that
      never drew the odd seed does not bend around it. That is why a forest is
      steadier than one deep tree.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="forest-t">
    <h2 id="forest-t">Choosing T, and an honest score</h2>
    <p>
      More trees make the vote steadier, and they never make the forest worse at
      learning; they only cost time. After a few dozen trees the answer hardly
      changes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every tree leaves out about a third of the seeds, which gives a free test.
      The score <strong>Right on unseen seeds</strong> asks, for each seed, only
      the trees that did not draw it. This is called the out-of-bag score, and
      it tells you how the forest does on seeds it has not learned from.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="forest-summary">
    <h2 id="forest-summary">The model in short</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Sample:</strong> each tree draws its own seeds, with repeats.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Grow:</strong> each question looks at one feature picked at
        random.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Vote:</strong> the forest answers with the most common answer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Check:</strong> test each seed on the trees that never saw it.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</div>

<style>
  .explain {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: var(--fs-16);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  section {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-4);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--fs-24);
  }

  .formula {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding-block: var(--s-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-18);
  }

  .symbols {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .symbols div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-4);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .symbols dt {
    font-family: 'KaTeX_Math', 'Times New Roman', serif;
    font-size: var(--fs-20);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .symbols dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.4em;
  }
</style>
